<DocsLayout title="Multi-Tenancy">
  <h1>Multi-Tenancy</h1>
  <p>
    Identity is <strong>single-tenant per deployment</strong>. The model is:
    one container, one user pool, one tenant ID. The
    <code>tenant_id</code> field on RPC messages is a defence-in-depth check
    against the configured default — not a tenant selector.
  </p>

  <h2>Why per-product, not per-customer</h2>
  <p>
    The cost of a fresh container is small (0.25 vCPU, 256 MB), and EntDB is
    already tenant-sharded. Running one Identity per product gives:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>User-pool isolation</strong> — Alice in product A cannot accidentally see product B's user list.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent JWT issuers</strong> — each product has its own <code>iss</code> claim, its own JWKS, its own key rotation cadence.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent OAuth client credentials</strong> — each product can register its own Google / Microsoft client.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent passkey RP ID</strong> — passkeys are scoped to a domain; products on different domains must have different RP IDs anyway.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The <code>tenant_id</code> field</h2>
  <p>
    Many RPC messages carry <code>tenant_id</code> for compatibility with
    multi-tenant graph DBs. Identity validates it equals
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code> and rejects the request
    otherwise. There is no path that lets a caller specify an arbitrary
    tenant.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing one EntDB across products</h2>
  <p>
    EntDB instances can host many tenants. Two Identity deployments
    pointing at the same EntDB stay isolated as long as they use
    distinct <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code> values:
  </p>
  <Code code={`# Product A
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb:50051 \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=product-a \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:0.1.0

# Product B
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb:50051 \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=product-b \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:0.1.0`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Type ID reservations</h2>
  <p>
    Identity reserves EntDB type IDs <code>1–99</code>. Other services
    sharing the same EntDB must use type IDs <code>100+</code>. The
    reservation is global (across tenants) because EntDB schemas are
    cluster-wide.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
